{
"Berkeley said that to be is to be perceived. Vasubandhu agreed, and then turned the same analysis on the perceiver.": "bbc972854317",
"Bishop Berkeley argued that the things we see and touch are collections of ideas, and that ideas exist only in being perceived. Their esse is percipi, nor is it possible they should have any existence out of the minds or thinking things which perceive them. He called it an opinion strangely prevailing amongst men, that houses, mountains, rivers, and in a word all sensible objects, have an existence, natural or real, distinct from their being perceived. Matter, he argued, is a notion no one can make sense of. The parallel with Vasubandhu is the closest between any Buddhist and any Western philosopher: the same thesis, the same attack on matter, the same appeal to dreams. They differ on what remains. Berkeleys world contains spirits, which are simple, active, enduring substances, and an eternal Spirit in whose mind things subsist when no one else perceives them. That is why the tree stays in the quad. Vasubandhu has no enduring souls and no God. For him the regularity of the world comes from seeds of past action ripening in streams of consciousness, and the streams are themselves processes.": "4fb9208bbe06",
"Consciousness-only and mind-only are heard in the West in two ways, both unfortunate. Some hear a denial that the world exists, and stop listening. Others hear the promise that thought creates reality, and expect to change their circumstances by wishing. Begin with something no one disputes. Two people walk into the same room, and one sees a party and the other an ordeal. A dog on the same street lives in a world of smells we do not have. What each of us meets is a world already shaped by the one who meets it. Then say what the teaching does with that fact. It asks where the shaping comes from, answers that it comes from habit and past action, and holds that it can be changed. Leave the question of whether there is a world apart from all minds for the end, and say frankly that Buddhists have disagreed about it.": "dc0e456aefc8",
"Even everything is mind is a tool. The texts say that if you hold on to it, you have not understood it.": "2b65e842ca5f",
"His Thirty Verses give the positive account. What we call self and things are figures of speech applied to the transformations of consciousness, of which there are three layers: a store consciousness that carries the seeds of past action, a level of thinking that takes the store as me, and the six kinds of sensory awareness. Experience has three natures. The imagined nature is the world as divided into a grasping subject and grasped objects, and this does not exist at all. The dependent nature is the flow of experience arising from conditions. The perfected nature is that same flow seen without the imagined division. The text ends with a warning about its own thesis. A practitioner who sets up some little thing before him and says, this is consciousness-only, still has an object and so does not dwell in consciousness-only. When knowledge finds nothing to grasp, both grasper and grasped fall away, and the basis of the mind is transformed.": "d8b91e064dbe",
"How you have acted shapes how you see. Change the one over time and the other changes.": "c9d8775d9ccf",
"Husserl founded phenomenology on a single methodical step. Set aside, without affirming or denying it, the belief that there is a world existing apart from consciousness, and describe experience exactly as it presents itself. What appears then is a structure. Every act of consciousness is of something, and the object as meant and the act that means it can be studied together. Husserl spent his life describing how the experienced world, with its stable things, its time, and its other people, is built up in layers of such acts. Many recent scholars think this is what Yogācāra is. On their reading vijñapti-mātra means nothing but presentation, a statement about what we have access to, and the three natures describe how experience is constituted. Husserls aim was a rigorous science of consciousness. The Yogācārins undertook the description in order to find where the division into subject and object is made, so that it could be unmade.": "e18d55683f62",
"In the fourth or fifth century the philosopher Vasubandhu made this into a thesis. He opens his Twenty Verses by citing a scripture that says the three realms are mind only, and explains: when consciousness arises, it appears as though there were outer objects, as a person with diseased eyes sees hairs and flies that are not there. He then answers the obvious objections. If there were no outer objects, why do things appear at a definite place and time, why do many people see the same thing, and why do things have effects? His reply uses two examples. In a dream we see villages and gardens at particular places and times, and dreams have bodily effects, though nothing outside is there. And hungry ghosts, who share a similar karma, together see a river as full of pus where human beings see water. Agreement among perceivers is explained by a likeness in the perceivers. He adds an argument against matter: an atom with no parts could not combine with others to make a body, and an atom with sides has parts and is no atom.": "e634753e644a",
"Kant rejected Berkeleys idealism and proposed another. Space, time, and causality are forms that the mind brings to experience. They are not features we read off from things. The world we know is therefore shaped throughout by the knower, and it is the only world we can know. He insisted against Berkeley that something independent of us supplies the material of experience, though we can say nothing of what it is in itself. Yogācāra agrees with Kant that the mind contributes structure, and its three layers of consciousness are an account of where that structure comes from. The difference is in the source. Kants forms are the same in every human mind and cannot be changed. Vasubandhus are habits laid down by past action and stored as seeds. They differ between kinds of beings, as the river of pus shows, and they can be transformed by practice.": "ba2b229e1dca",
"On the purpose all three Western views stop short in the same way. Berkeley wanted to defeat skepticism and atheism. Kant wanted to secure the foundations of science, and Husserl wanted to found a science of consciousness. Each leaves the subject of experience in place: a soul, a transcendental ego, a pure consciousness. Yogācāra directs its analysis at that subject. The grasper is as much a construction as the grasped, and consciousness-only, held as a doctrine, is one more object for a grasper to hold. The texts say so in as many words, and they describe the end of the path as a transformation of the basis in which neither side of the division is found. The school that is most often called Buddhist idealism ends by denying the ego that idealism rests on.": "cee1945a2ec1",
"The answer depends on which Yogācāra one has in view. If the school is read as denying that anything exists outside consciousness, Berkeley is closest, and the Twenty Verses could be bound with his Principles. If it is read as an inquiry into how experience is constructed, Husserl is closest in method and Kant in the idea that the mind supplies the framework. The texts support both readings in different places, and the tradition itself divided over the question. This lesson takes no side. For the purpose of the teaching it matters less than it seems.": "146fc0d2915a",
"The first further step is the moral one, and it has no counterpart in Berkeley, Kant, or Husserl. In Vasubandhus example the ghosts see pus because of what they have done. The world a being lives in is the ripening of its past action, stored as seeds and coming to fruit as a way of perceiving. Beings with like histories perceive alike, which is why human beings agree about the river. The claim joins the theory of knowledge to ethics. On this account the world one meets is in part the trace of what one has been, and changing how one acts changes, in time, what one finds. A milder version can be checked in ordinary life. The irritable person lives among irritating people, and the generous person finds the same street friendlier. The schools psychology, with its lists of mental factors and its account of how habits are laid down and uprooted, is an attempt to work this out in detail.": "69afbdc56a38",
"The point is practical. The sense of a me in here facing things out there is something the mind builds, and it can be seen through.": "00623591c2f2",
"The roots of the teaching are in the earliest discourses. Intention is the leader of things; intention is first, theyre made by intention, begins the Dhammapada. Asked to teach the all, the Buddha answered: Its just the eye and sights, the ear and sounds, the nose and smells, the tongue and tastes, the body and touches, and the mind and ideas. Anyone who claimed to describe some other all would be out of his element. And to a deity who had tried to reach the end of the world by traveling, he said: it is in this fathom-long carcass with its perception and mind that I describe the world, its origin, its cessation, and the practice that leads to its cessation. In each passage the world that matters for the path is the world as experienced.": "c466808cfe52",
"The second step is the schools refusal to rest in its own thesis, which was quoted above. Western idealisms are positions, and their authors defended them as true descriptions of how things are. The Thirty Verses treat consciousness-only as a stage. A meditator first sees that the objects of experience are presentations, and so stops grasping at them. He then sees that a presentation with nothing presented is no presentation, and stops grasping at mind as well. What is left is described only by negation: without grasping, beyond thought, a knowledge that is not of the world as divided. The later tradition argued over whether this goes beyond what Nāgārjuna had said or restates it with a psychology added. For the practitioner the instruction is the same. The idea that everything is mind is a ladder of the kind met in the last three lessons.": "4b00a31480fc",
"This school does not say the world is unreal. It says that the world you meet has already been shaped by your mind.": "bc041c004df1",
"Where a human being sees water, a fish sees a home. The traditions own example is harsher: a hungry ghost sees pus.": "c2f519af1f05"
}
